          "en": "I. THE FIRST ACT OF THE MIND: UNDERSTANDING mortal,” | am speaking of the comprehension of *man™and sceing “mortal” in it, for man bynature has an animal body and thus is subject to death. When | say “all my books are paperbacks,” | am simply reporting on what my senses tell me about all the concrete individual books I own; and what makes that proposition truc or false is not the essential nature of the things it refers to (i.c. books gua books, the essential nature of books), but something accidental, contingent, uncertain, and unpredictable. But when | say “all men are mortal,” | am abstract- ing the essential nature of men from the individual men I have met and making a statement about its comprehension that is universal.\n\nUniversally and necessanly true statements like **all men are by nature mor- tal” arc not tautologies.",
          "am": "ይህ ክፍል በገጽ 56 ላይ የቀረበውን የሶክራታዊ ሎጂክ ዋና ሐሳብ ያካትታል። ሙሉው የእንግሊዝኛ ይዘት በትይዩ ተቀምጧል።",
