          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 58 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "(3) Terms are either vague or exact.\n\nVague terms ar¢ not necessarily ambiguous or unclear. “Tall” is a vague term (‘six feet tall” is an exact term) but “tall” is neither ambiguous nor unclear.\n\nThere is nothing necessarily wrong with a vague term. We often need vague terms rather than exact terms; they are very useful. For much of our knowledge IS not exact, so the terms that express that knowledge rightly cannot be exact either. We often need a “fuzzy logic™in our terms.\n\nBut although there is room for “fuzzy logic™in terms, there is no room for “fuzzy logic” in propositions or arguments. Propositions arc cither true or false and arguments are cither valid or invalid; and there is no third possibility and no fuzziness or sliding scale or matter of degree between true and false, or between valid and invalid.\n\nHowever, we often cannot be certain whether a given proposition is true or false; and rhar dimension (namcly, probability) can sometimes be “fuzzy.” At other times, that dimension can be exact, as in statistics. In statistics, even inex- actness can be exact: e.g. a “5% margin of crror.” (4) Termsare cither univocal, equivocal, or analogical. A univocal term has one and only one meaning. An equivocal term has two or more quite different and unrelated meanings. An analogical term has two or more meanings that arc (a) partly the same and partly different, and (b) related to each other.\n\nWhen | say “I ate two apples” and “You ate two hamburgers,” | use “ate” and \"two\" univocally. When 1 say “The river has two banks™and “The town has two banks,” | use “banks” equivocally, for there is no connection between a river bank and a money bank. When I say “The good man gave his good dog a good meal,” | use “good” analogically, for there is at the same time a similarity and a",
          "am": "የገጽ 58 ሁለተኛ ክፍል ውይይት እና ማብራሪያ።",
          "notes": "Peter Kreeft, Socratic Logic (Ed. 3.1), Page 58 (Part 2)."
