          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Classifying terms difference between a good man, a good dog, and a good meal. All three are desir- able, but a good man is wise and moral, a good dog is tame and affectionate, and a good mcal is tasty and nounshing. But a good man 1s not tasty and nourishing, cxcept to a cannibal; a good dog is not wise and moral, except in cartoons, and a good mcal is not tamc and affectionate, unless it's alive as you cat it.\n\nStrictly speaking, a term is never univocal, equivocal, or analogical in itself; it is only used univocally, equivocally, or analogically. The phrases “bark of a dog” and “bark of a tree” use “bark” equivocally; the phrases “bark of an oak™ and “bark ofa maple” use “bark” univocally. (So do “bark of a hound™and “bark of a poodic.”) “Hcalthy food” and “healthy exercise” use “healthy” univocally (for both mean something that causes health in a human body), but “a healthy climate.” “a healthy body.” and “a healthy sweat” use “healthy analogically, for a healthy climate is a cause of a healthy body, while a healthy sweatis an effect of a healthy body. ‘Exercise’ is an action, ‘sweat’ is a substance, ‘climate’ is nei- ther.\n\nOnc ofthe things computers cannot do is understand and use analogies. One of the things philosophers and poets do especially well is to understand and use analogies. See also “arguments by analogy,” p. 329.\n\nExcrciscs: Classify each of the underlined terms as univocal, cquivocal, or ana- logical.\n\nI love ice cream and I love you too.\n\nTo murder is evil, and to be murdered is also evil.\n\nA litter of pups was living in the street in the middle of a pile of |itter.\n\nTwo customers paid two hundred dollars each for two chairs two days ago.\n\nAfter I digest this logic course, I'm going to digest my dinner.\n\nMacbeth murdered Banquo, but he didn't murder the English language as you do.",
