          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Il. TERMS (10) Terms designating groups of things are used cither collectively or divi- sively. When | use a term collectively, | mean the group as a whole; when | use a term divisively, I mean cach individual member of the group. A collective term refers to a number of individuals looked at as a single group. like the soldiers in an army or the crew ofa ship.\n\nOnly terms designating groups can be cither collective or divisive. Which of the two it is, is determined by use, by how the term is used in a sentence. For instance. “library” is used collectively in the sentence “This library 1s composed of ten thousand books,” but the same term is used divisively in the sentence “This town has three libranes.” When | say “this class is the smartest logic class I've ever taught,” I use “class” collectively, because 1 don’t mean that every sin- gle member of the class is smart, only that the class as a whole is. But when | say “all men are mortal.” I usc “men” divisively because | mean that every sin- gle man is mortal, not just that the species homo sapiens, or humanity, is mortal as a species.\n\nExercise: Tell whether each underlined term is used collectively or divisively.\n\nI. That is a tall pile of bricks.\n\n2. The trees on Holly Hill make a fine sight.\n\n3. Nonc of my philosophy courses is casy.\n\n4. The marbles in this bag weigh five pounds.",
