          "id": "p68-1",
          "speaker": "Page 68: Il. TERMS",
          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 68፦ Il. TERMS",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Il. TERMS pragmatic logicians allow them to be applied also to singular terms like “Socrates” —- e.g. “rational”is the specific difference of“*Socrates™as well as the specific difference of “man.” The theoretical dispute seems unimportant for pur- poscs of practical logic.\n\nA clarification regarding the wo uses of the term “accident”: The same word, “accident,” is uscd in logic both for nine of the ten categonces (all of them except “substance™are “‘accidents™) and for one of the five predicables. Do not confusc categories with predicables; rememberthat the categories classify terms absolutely, in themselves, while the predicables classify terms relatively, as rela- tions of a predicate to a subject in a proposition.\n\nHowever, individuals cannot be predicate terms in strict logical form, since the predicate is some aspect of the nature of the subject. and individuals are not (universal) natures. They have natures. Thus, “The murderer is Lucretia™must be translated into “Lucrctia is the murderer” before we can speak of a predica- blc relationship (here, accident). “Clark Kent is Superman™is not a proposition with a predicable relationship at all but an equation between two names. Here “is” is the same as =\" in math. That is nor its usual meaning: sce p. 140.",
          "am": "ይህ ክፍል በገጽ 68 ላይ የቀረበውን የሶክራታዊ ሎጂክ ዋና ሐሳብ ያካትታል። ሙሉው የእንግሊዝኛ ይዘት በትይዩ ተቀምጧል።",
          "notes": "Peter Kreeft, Socratic Logic (Ed. 3.1), Page 68."
