          "en": "political systems, or moral systems as #22, above, did for religions.\n\nOutlining The principles of outlining arc like the principles of morality in that they are usually simple and easy to understand but hard to obey. They are not hard in themselves; we are just lazy! But their practice has a big payoff. Few things clar- ify and improve writing and thinking more. By far the most important rule about outlining is: JUST DO IT!\n\nThe basic rules are thesc:\n\nI. Titles and subtitles are not parts of the outline but placed above it.\n\n2. Use Roman numcrals for main topics, capital letters for main subtopics, then Arabic numerals for sub-subtopics, then small letters, then Arabic numer- als in parentheses. then small letters in parentheses.\n\n3. For each number orletter there must be a topic. Each must stand on a line by itself. E.g. never write “Al” or “lA.” 4. There must always be more than one subtopic under any topic. (No A without a B, no | without a 2.) 5. A subtopic must be placed under the main topic which it qualifies. This 1s the rule that gives many students the most trouble:\n\ndistinguishing the relative rank of each point, deciding which are coordinate with which, and which are subordinate to which. This is an ability that is more intuitive than teachable; but it can be greatly improved by practice. and cannot be greatly improved without practice.\n\n6. Subtopics arc indented, so that all numbers or letters of the same kind come directly under cach other vertically.",
