          "role": "prose",
          "en": "III; Material Fallacies First, we must distinguish maternal fallacies, which are covered in this chapter, from formal fallacies, which arc covered later. Formal fallacies arc mistakes in reasoning, errors in the operation of the third act of the mind. For instance, “Some men are mortal, and some mortals are fish, therefore some men are fish™ commits a formal fallacy. Some men are fish™does not logically follow from the two premises that “‘some men arc mortal” and “some mortals are fish,” even though both the premises are true. There is no ambiguity or wrong use of terms in this argument, only bad reasoning. We will lca the rules of good logical rea- soning in the third section ofthis book, which covers the third act of the mind.\n\nMatenal fallacies, on the other hand, are treated here, in the scction of the book which covers the first act of the mind, because they are mistakes in under- standing the meaning or usc of terms, errors in the operation of the first act of the mind. These matcnal fallacies are found in the course of an argument, so they arc called “fallacies,” or mistakes in rcasoning; but they are not mistakes in the logical form but mistakes in the content or matter or meaning.\n\nMost of the errors and misunderstandings that plague our conversation and argumentation come from a loosc use of language rather than from formal fal- lacies. Formally fallacious arguments don’t deccive us as often as matenally fal- lacious arguments do. Therefore we have made this section, on material falla- cies, longer than it is in most logic texts. Most logic texts do not do this because they specialize in what they do best: the clear.",
