          "en": "Fallacies oflanguage D. The Fallacy of Misplaced Concreteness (Confusing the Abstract with the Concrete) 11] E. Confusing the Logical, Psychological, and Physical “Because™112 F. The Existential Fallacy (Confusing Essence and Existence) G. Confusing the Natural with the Common Short Story: “Love Is a Fallacy” by Max Schulman (from The Many Loves ofDobie Gillis) Section 1. Fallacies of language 1A. Equivocation Equivocation is the simplest and most common of all the material fallacies. It means simply that the same term is used in two or more different senses in the course of an argument.\n\nFallacics, in the logical sense, occur only in arguments.\n\nIn ordinary lan- guage the word “fallacy”is often used for any kind of mistake, but in logic a fal- lacy is a mistake tn argument. A fallacious argument 1s one that seems to prove its conclusion but does not, for one of two reasons: material (the use of terms) or formal (the reasoning process). Almost any terms, except numbers, have the potential to be used equivocally. But no term is equivocal in itself; a term is only used cquivocally. This happens when it changes its meaning in the course of an argument.\n\nConcepts arc not equivocal, only terms (i.e. the words that express them).",
          "am": "ይህ ክፍል በገጽ 81 ላይ የቀረበውን የሶክራታዊ ሎጂክ ዋና ሐሳብ ያካትታል። ሙሉው የእንግሊዝኛ ይዘት በትይዩ ተቀምጧል።",
