          "en": "Il. MATERIAL FALLACIES IB, Amphiboly An “amphiboly™is not an ambiguous word (or phrasc) but ambiguous syntax (word order or grammatical structure). Simple puns are based on equivocation; slightly longer “language jokes™are usually based on amphiboly, c.g. What word is pronounced incorrectly by nearly all English people? Answer: the word “incorrectly.” Here 1s an example of amphiboly that is not a joke. just an ambiguity:\n\n“Aristotle the peripatetic (i.c. the ‘walker’) taught his students walking.” There are two ambiguities here. First, who was walking, Aristotle or his students?\n\nSecond, did he teach them the art of walking or did he teach them something else while he was walking?\n\nThe 18th-century philosopher Berkeley was trying to prove his conclusion that esse est percipi, “to be is to be perceived.” i.e. that there is no “material world” no objective reality independent of perception or awareness, whether mental or sensory. He used this argument: “Is it not a great contradiction to think a thing exists when you do not think it?\" The ambiguity here comes from the fact that the adverbial phrase “when you do not think it” can modify either the verb “think” or the verb “cxists.” Only the first interpretation 1s “a great contradic- tion,” for it means “to think .",
