          "en": "1C, Accent Here the ambiguity comes from voice inflection, ironic or sarcastic tone, or even facial expression, or innuendo. (If you don’t know what “innuendo” means, look it up. Every literate person should have — and. more importantly, usc — a dic- tionary.) Accent and amphiboly arc the two fallacies that are almost always humorous, usually ironic, and often sarcastic.\n\nNotice, in the following two examples, how the same sentence can have many significantly different meanings by implication if different words arc emphasized:\n\n“I do not choose to run at this time.” (But pcrhaps he will.) “1 do not choose to run at this time.” (no ambiguity) “I do not choose to run at this time.” (But | can be forced.) “1 do not choose to run at this time.” (But I can be drafted.) “1 do not choose to run at this time.” (But I may do it tomorrow.) Another, similar example:\n\n“#2 don’t have to tell the whole truth, you know.” (But others do.) “We don t haveto tell the whole truth, you know.” (You only think we do.) “We don't save to tell the whole truth, you know.” (It’s optional.)",
