          "en": "Fallacies oflanguage description of it - e.g. calling an idea “up-to-date” or “wild,” “traditional” or “stagnant,” “flexible” or “fickle.” This is equivocation or double meaning because it both describes and cvaluates at once, in a single word. It both denotes a fact and connotes an cvaluating attitude toward the fact. That is why it is clas- sificd under the heading of fallacies of cquivocation.\n\nThe most obvious and egregious examples of slanting are in propaganda (which has been defined by a famous punster as the proper mate for a proper goose). But slanting can also be done negatively, by omitting relevant informa- tion, and by sclecting only favorable, or only unfavorable, data. This form of slanting is much subtler and harder to detect. The old U.S.S.R.’s official news- paper Pravda was full of blatant propaganda:\n\nbut even (“‘even™) Amencan newspapers habitually slant by omission, sometimes as part of deliberate edito- rial policy, e.g. by giving candidates of its favored political party more coverage, and the opposing candidates less, and reversing this when it comes to scandals or accusations.",
