          "en": "“Poisoning the Well” is the dircct attack on the trustworthiness of the per- son making a statement instead of addressing the statement, e.g.: “*How can you believe anything he says? He .\n\n. .” The fallacy docs not consist in criticizing the person’s character or reliability; that is certainly a relevant consideration, and it would be a fallacy to suppress it, or any other relevant evidence. The fallacy con- sists in substituting the personal attack for facing the person's argument or truth- claim, using it as a “‘rcason” for not looking at the facts or rcasons.\n\n“Poisoning the Well” usually (but not always) involves another fallacy also, viz. slanting or name-calling within the attack on the person's character, the usc of “question-begging epithets” or insulting labels instead of facts and reasons for doubting his reliability. If you believe your opponent's character or reliabili- ty is questionable, it is legitimate to say so, but reasons must be given for that belicf too. not just insults.\n\n“Tu quoque” (literally, “you 100\") consists of accusing your critic of the same thing yourcritic accuses you of, rather than defending yourself against the criticism. “I’ve just proved that you're a liar. Refute my argument.” “Well, you're just as much a har as | am.” Perhaps he is, but that does not refute his argument.\n\nThe most common form of ad hominem today is “the genetic fallacy,” which consists in “refuting” an idca by showing some suspicious psychological",
