          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 92 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Chesterton satirizes the uncritical refusal to accept anything on authority in the first sentence of his autobiography:\n\n“Bowing down in blind credulity. as is my custom, before mere authonty, and the tradition ofthe elders, superstitiously swallowing a story | could not test at the time by experiment or private judgment, I am firmly of the opinion that I was born on the 29th of May, 1874, on Campden Hill, Kensington, and baptized according to the formularies of the Church of England in the little church of St. George opposite the large Waterworks that dominated that ridge.” However, humans arc fallible, and therefore, according to the medieval maxim, “the argument from (human) authority is thc weakest of all arguments.” As we grow, we question our authorities and test them by reason and try to learn more and more on our own (though no onc cver gets to the point where he no longer needs to rely on any authontics at all).\n\nThe appeal to authonty becomes fallacious when the authority is (1) irrel- evant (¢.g. when a movie star is taken as an authority on science, or a priest on howto make a lot of money); or (2) unreliable (e.g. the “National Enquirer” or “Pravda™); or (3) unnecessary, since there is an argument from reason instead that is casy.,clear. and readily available; or (4) when the appcal is dogmatic, i.c.\n\nclosed rather than open, claiming certainty rather than probability; when men are treated as gods; or (5) when the appeal is uncritical, when there is no good reason why this authonty should be trusted. This latter form includes such forms as the “snowjob,” the “appeal to the expert,” and the “appeal to Big Names.” Examples: (Which,if any, of the five errors above does cach of the follow- ing commit?) (1) “The doctrine of Aristotle is the supreme truth, because his intellect was the limit of the human intellect. It is therefore rightly said that he was created",
