          "role": "prose",
          "en": "The “other side of the coin.” or correlative. of the appealto fearis the appeal to desire. This has not yet received a separate technical name; yet the thought that “It's true because want it to be true” is perhaps the commonest fallacy of all. Frcud would certainly say so. (Is that an argumentum ad verecundiam?) Examples:\n\n(1) “Of course there's a rcal Santa Claus, but he doesn’t bning presents to children who don’t belicve in him.” (2) “Before you answer, remember who pays your salary.” (3) Chairman of the Board: *‘All those in favor of my proposal, say ‘I agree’; all those opposed, say *I resign.”” (4) “Thomas, if you could only sce your way clear to agree with King Henry's theology. you would be Chancellor of England for life.” (5) “If you just cant agree with the reasonableness of company policy on this issue, you can start looking for another job.” (6) “It is reasonable to believe in God because if you believe in God, you have your best chance to be rewarded with Heaven. If you do not, you have a good chance to be punished with Hell.” (Is “Pascal's Wager™a fallacy?)",
