          "en": "ll. MATERIAL FALLACIES (7) “If you adopted the belief that everywhere is Heaven, even here, and everyone is God. even you, you would be blissfully happy and free fromfear. For whatis there to fear in Heaven, and what does God have to be afraid of?” (8) (Advisors to their campaigning politician): “The best argument we know for changing your mind on this controversial issue is the polls, whichtell us that your constituents will not re-elect you unless you agree with them about it.” 2D. Ad misericordiam The fallacy of“ad misericordiam,” or “appeal to pity” is the perversion of some- thing that is perfectly legitimate in itself, just as is the “appeal to authority.” Pity is usually a good thing. often appropriate and sometimes nccessary: but it can- not be a substitute for argument.\n\nThe commonest form of this fallacy in tcachers’ experience is students’ attempt to “buy” higher grades or more time to complcte late assignments, because of the supposed suffering that they experienced either before the test or assignment (“Give me an A even though | scored a 70% on your test because | studied terribly hard all night”) or afterwards (“If you flunk me, my parents will be devastated. They sacnficed all their savings on my tuition.”).",
