          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 97 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "The saying “the exception proves the rule” is a (rather sloppy and mislead- ing) way of refuting this fallacy. What that saying really means is not, literally, that an exception like “some triangles are not three-sided” proves the rule that “all triangles are three-sided.” or that “this man is ten feet tall” proves the truth of the rule that *'no man is ten feet tall.” That would be absurd and self-contra- dictory. What it means is that the exception or special case presupposes the rule.\n\nIf there is no rule. there can be no exceptions to it. Most rules are generaliza- tions that arc only usually true, and admit exceptions or special cases; e.g. “It’s wrong to take another man’s property against his will,” which is nor true when the other man is about to commit suicide with his own gun. “Boston gets more snow than Charlotte, North Carolina™is true only 99 years out of 100, but not always.\n\nExamplcs:\n\n(1) “The Vatican allowed a convent of nuns in Italy who knewthey were about to be raped by Nazi soldicrs to take birth control pills to protect them from getting pregnant. Therefore the Church doesn’t really think contraception is wrong.” (2) “There arc a lot of idiots who can’t pass a logic course. Therefore man IS not rational.” (3) “Why don’t you get under the covers and go to sleep? What are you doing?” “I'm making lizard check.” \"Do you do that often?” **I've doneit every night for forty years.” “Why?” “When | was a kid in Boy Scout camp, somebody put a lizard in my bed, and it bit me. | want to catch the next lizard before that happens again.” (4) “If women ran the world, we'd have fewer wars.” “Oh yeah? Lizzie Borden was a multiple axe murderer. That goes to show you how aggressive women are.” 3C, Composition This fallacy consists in arguing from the part to the whole,ignoring the fact that whatis true of the part is not necessarily true of the whole.",
