          "en": "Enlightenment in such a loud voice that the pony shicd.\n\n“I beg your pardon,” said John.\n\n“Eh?” said Mr. Enlightenment.\n\n“1 don’t quite understand,” said John.\n\n“Why, it’s as plain as a pikestaff,” said the other. “Your people in Punitania believe in the Landlord because they have not had the benefits of a scientific training. For example, now, 1 dare say it would be news to you to hear that the carth was round —- round as an orange, my lad!” “Well, I don’t know that it would,” said John, fecling a little disappointed.\n\n“My father always said it was round.” “No. no, my dcar boy,” said Mr. Enlightenment, ‘You must have misunder- stood him. It is well known that everyone in Puritania thinks the carth flat. It is not likely that [ should be mistaken on such a point. Indeed. it is out of the ques- tion. Then again, there is the paleontological evidence.” “What's that?” “Why. they tell you in Punitania that the Landlord made all these roads. But that is quite impossible, for old people can remember the time when the roads were not ncarly so good as they are now, and what is more, scientists have found all over the country traces of old roads running in quite different directions. The inference is obvious.” John said nothing.\n\n“I said,” repeated Mr. Enlightenment, “that the inference was obvious.” “Oh, yes, yes, of course,” said John hastily, turning a little red. (Pilgrims Regress)",
