          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 118 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "It becomes a matter of logic when, in debate. the original strategy is implic- itly changed. E.g. in court a prosecuting attorncy may badger the defense to prove its case as if the accused were guilty until proved innocent rather than innocent until proved guilty; or a moralist crusading for prohibition may demand proof that alcohol contributes to the health of bodies or socictics.\n\nIn a debate about a controversial practice that used to be illegal or unavailable, such as cloning or surrogate motherhood, the onc who attacks the new procedure often assumes that the burden of proof is on the “new kid on the block,” on the new permissiveness, while the onc who defends it often assumes that any practice, like a person,is innocent until proved guilty. Who has the burden of proof here 1s itself a matter of scnous argument, but this should be agreed on before argu- ment proceeds. and whoever assumes the burden of proof should not “cop out™ on giving such a proof (i.c. proving his case) by simply accusing his opponent of not proving his case.\n\n6G. Winning the Argument but Losing the Arguer — or Vice Versa “Winning the argument but losing the arguer” means ignoring the personal, psy- chological factor and ending up being distrusted and treated as an enemy or a threat by the person you wanted to persuade. Good logic is an instrument of per- suasion; it is only one ingredient in a larger interpersonal situation; and those who love logic and are good at it often forget this context. Intelligent people’s minds are not changed by bad logic, but they are not changed by bad personal tactics cither.",
