          "en": "perfect, absolute realities.\n\n[t is reasonable to hold that these Forms exist, indeed, as ideas in the mind of God; but not in themsclves, as independent entities or substances. It is also superfluous, as Aristotle pointed out, to supposc that whenever there exist two of any kind of thing (c.g. two men) there exists also a third thing, viz. the com- mon essence. There is indeed a common essence. but that essence is not a “third man.” If it were, there would have to be a fourth “man” common to the “third man” and the other two men. er cetera et cetera ad infinitum.\n\n“The love of Humanity” can also be a fallacy of misplaced concreteness if the abstraction “humanity” substitutes for concrete individuals as objects of love. This is what Dickens calls “telescopic philanthropy” (in Bleak House).\n\nI Though both are controversial, and some would defend these two examples as not falla- cious at all.",
