          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Ill. MATERIAL FALLACIES substituting concern for faraway groups like “the poor” and abstractions like “justice in the Near East” for concrete human beings and problems in our own familics and lives. As Linus says in a “Peanuts” comic stnp, “1 have no problem with Humanity. It’s people I can’t stand.” Or as G.K. Chesterton put it, O how I love Humanity With love so pure and pringlish, And howI hate the hornd French, Who never will be English!\n\nWhen we treat abstractions like “causality” and “substance™as concrete entities, we become subject to cntiques like Hume's, in which he points out that no onc cver observed “causality,” or “substance.” only particular things and events. Even the self 1s not an observable object that we can catch by a kind of inner observation. Hume erroncously deduced from this premise the conclusion that there was no such thing as causality or substance or even any substantial self. This is itself a fallacy: a non sequitur. But his crror was provoked by the fallacy of misplaced concreteness on the part of those who thought of causality as a kind of river of force, or substance as a kind of foundation for the building of accidents, or the self as a kind of ghost or “little man inside pulling strings.” (Thus Hume's critique was also an example of the “straw man” fallacy.) 7E, Confusing Logical, Physical, and Psychological Causes Sce p. 81, paragraph 1, for this fallacy.\n\nTF. Confusing Essence and Existence (The Existential Fallacy) One example of this fallacy is rather rare and csotenc: Sartre's claim that man’s essence Is simply to exist, that we arbitrarily construct our own essence.",
          "am": "ይህ ክፍል በገጽ 122 ላይ የቀረበውን የሶክራታዊ ሎጂክ ዋና ሐሳብ ያካትታል። ሙሉው የእንግሊዝኛ ይዘት በትይዩ ተቀምጧል።",
          "notes": "Peter Kreeft, Socratic Logic (Ed. 3.1), Page 122."
