          "en": "Il. MATERIAL FALLACIES “Love Is a Fallacy” by Max Schulman (from The Many Loves of Dobie Gillis) Cool was | and logical. Keen. calculating, perspicacious, acute and astute- I was all of these. My brain was as powerful as a dynamo, as precise as a chemist’s scales, as penetrating as a scalpel. And - think of it! - [ was only cight- cen.\n\nIt is not often that one so young has such a giant intellect. Take, for cxam- ple, Petey Bellows, my roommate at the university. Same age, same background, but dumb as an ox. A nicc cnough fellow, you understand, but nothing upstairs.\n\nEmotional type. Unstable. Impressionable. Worst ofall. a faddist. Fads, | submit, are the very negation of reason. To be swept up in every new craze that comes along, to surrender yourself to idiocy just because everybody clse is doing it — this, to me,is the acme of mindlcssness. Not, however, to Petey.\n\nOnc afternoon | found Pctcy lying on his bed with an expression of such distress on his face that | immediately diagnosed appendicitis. “Don’t move,” | said. “Don’t take a laxative. I'll get a doctor.” “Raccoon,” he mumbled thickly.\n\n“Raccoon?” | said, pausing in my flight.\n\n“| want a raccoon coat,” he wailed.",
