          "en": "Il. MATERIAL FALLACIES “Look,” I said to Petey when | got back Monday morning. | threw open the suitcase and revealed the huge, hairy, gamy object that my father had worn in his Stutz Bearcat in 1928.\n\n“Holy Toledo!\"said Petey reverently. He plunged his hands into the raccoon coat and then his face. “Holy Toledo!™he repeated fifteen or twenty times.\n\n“Would you like 11?\" | asked.\n\n“Oh yes!™he cried. clutching the greasy pelt to him. Then a canny look came into his eyes. “What do you want for it?” “Your girl,\" | said, mincing no words.\n\n“Polly?” he said in a horrified whisper. “You want Polly?” “That's nght.” He flung the coat from him. “Never,” he said stoutly.\n\n1 shrugged. “Okay. If you don’t want to be in the swim, | guess it’s your business.” I sat down in a chair and pretended to read a book, but out of the corner of my cye | kept watching Petey. He was a torn man. First he looked at the coat with an cxpression of a waif at a bakery window. Then he turned away and sct his jaw resolutely. Then he looked back at the coat, with even morc longing in his face.\n\nThen he turned away. but with not so much resolution this time. Back and forth his head swiveled, desire waxing, resolution waning. Finally he didn’t turn away at all; he just stood and stared with mad lust at the coat.\n\n“It isn't as though | was in love with Polly,” he said thickly. “Or going stcady or anything like that.” “That's nght,” | murmured.\n\n“What's Polly to me, or me to Polly?” “Not a thing,\" said |.",
