          "id": "p128-2",
          "speaker": "Page 128 (Continued)",
          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 128 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "“Of course you are. Because when the premises of an argument contradict cach other, there can be no argument. If there is an irresistible force, there can bc no immovable object. If there is an immovable object, there can be no irre- sistible force. Get 11?” “Tell me some more of this keen stuff,” she said eagerly.\n\nI consulted my watch. “1 think we'd better call it a night. I'll take you home now, and you go over all the things you've learned. We'll have another session tomorrow night.” I deposited her at the girls’ dormitory, where she assured me that she had had a perfectly ternf evening, and 1 went glumly home to my room. Petey lay snoring in his bed. the raccoon coat huddled like a great hairy beast at his feet.\n\nFor a moment | considered waking him and telling him that he could have his girl back. It seemed clear that my project was doomed to failure. The girl sim- ply had a logic-proof head.\n\nBut then | reconsidered. | had wasted one evening:\n\n1 might as well waste another. Who knew? Maybe somewhere in the extinct crater of her mind a few embers still smoldered.\n\nMaybe somehow | could fan them into flame.\n\nAdmittedly it was not a prospect fraught with hope, but I decided to give it one more (ry.\n\nSeated under the oak the next evening | said, “Our first fallacy tonight is called Ad Miscncordiam.”",
          "am": "የገጽ 128 ሁለተኛ ክፍል ውይይት እና ማብራሪያ።",
