          "en": "“Love Is a Fallacy” She quivered with delight.\n\n“Listen closely,” 1 said. “A man applies for a job. When the boss asks him what his qualifications are, he replies he has a wife and six children at home,the wife is a helpless cnpple, the children have nothing to eat, no clothes to wear, no shoes on their feet, there are no beds in the house, no coal in the cellar, and winter is coming.” A tear rolled down cach of Polly's pink cheeks. “Oh, this 1s awful, awful,” she sobbed.\n\n“Yes, it is awful,” I agreed, “but it's no argument. The man never answered the boss's question about his qualifications. Instead, he appealed to the boss's sympathy. He committed the fallacy of Ad Misericordiam. Do you understand?” “Have you got a handkerchief?” she blubbered.\n\nI handed her a handkerchief and tried to keep from screaming while she wiped her eyes. “Next,” | said in a carefully controlled tone, “‘we will discuss Falsc Analogy. Here is an example: Students should be allowed to look at their textbooks during examinations. After all, surgeons have X rays to guide them duning an operation. lawyers have briefs to guide them duning a tnal, carpenters have blueprints to guide them when they are building a house.",
          "am": "ይህ ክፍል በገጽ 129 ላይ የቀረበውን የሶክራታዊ ሎጂክ ዋና ሐሳብ ያካትታል። ሙሉው የእንግሊዝኛ ይዘት በትይዩ ተቀምጧል።",
          "notes": "Peter Kreeft, Socratic Logic (Ed. 3.1), Page 129."
