          "en": "Properties can substitute for specific differences, but then we have a defi- nition by property instcad of by essence: c.g. “Man is the animal that writes™; “tnangles are planc figures whose interior angles add up to 180 degrees.” A reviewof the predicables (page 56) may be necessary here. It is essential to remember that “species” does not mean “any man-made classification at all in which a more general class 1s subdivided into more specific classes.” A thing's “species” is its essence. or, in Anistotle’s words. “what 1s predicated essentially of many different individuals’: c.g. Socrates, Plato, and Aristotle are all men.\n\n(They arc all Greeks too, but that is not essential.) And a “genus” is nor just “any man-made gencral class,” but the common part ofthe essence, or in Anstotle’s words, “what is predicated essentially of scv- eral things that differ in species’: men, monkeys, dogs. sharks. guppies. clams, robins. hawks. snails, possums, alligators, and snakes arc all animals.\n\nFinally, a “specific difference” is the differentiating part of the essence, or, in Anstotle’s words. “what makes one species different from another within a genus.” Man is the only rational animal; tangles the only rthree-sided enclosed plane figures. etc.\n\nThe species is the complete essence, including both the genus and the specific difference.\n\nIn terms of a term's comprehension, the genus is onc part of the",
