          "en": "(1) What is infinite cannot be defined, for a “definition” (de-finio) of a thing means telling the limits or boundarics of that thing, and whatis infinite has no limits or boundaries. The only definition possible for something infinite is a negative one. Thus *‘cternity” means “not-time,” and “infinity” itself means “not-finitude.” It is, of course, impossible to define God. if God is infinite being or an infinite being or the infinite being. (However, it is possible to define finite gods, like the Greek Olympians. But see point 2 below.) As far as God is concerned, we can only say (a) what God is not or (b) what God is like. But this is not to define God. for cach of these two choices violates a rule of definition. (a) If we usc literal, univocal language, we can onlysay what God is not, not what God is. (E.g. God is not a man, God is not a body, God is not in time.) But God is not a negative thing, like nonbeing or death or evil. So a negative definition of God would violate one of the rules of definition (rule #5). (b) If we use analogical or metaphorical language, we can speak positively.\n\nbut only to say what God is like, not what God is,literally. (E.g. we can say that God is a Father, God is Love, God is a King. But God is not a human biological father or the changeable human passion of love, or an carthly political ruler.) And of course non-literal language violates another rule of definition (rule #3).",
