          "role": "prose",
          "en": "V. THE SECOND ACT OF THE MIND:\n\nJUDGMENT Thisis the simple and obvious core ofthe art of reading, and this is onc of the most uscful parts oflogic. For the complaints that students can no longer read scem to be increasing every gencration. After a century of universal public education. Amencans do not know howto read or write as well today as they did a hundred years ago. If you doubt this, compare newspapers or elementary read- ing textbooks for any grade a century ago with those today.\n\nOne ofthe best remedics for bad reading and writing is good logic, espe- cially the analysis of propositions. For thinking clcarly, expressing your thoughts clearly (in wnting or speaking), and interpreting another’s expressions (written or spoken) clearly are three arts that arc very closely allied; no one of them can be done well without doing the other two. And the part of logic that is most directly related to this is the part that studies propositions.\n\nSubject and Predicate After the mind has understood the nature or essence or “whatness™of some- thing by forming a concept.\n\nit then goes on to relate two of these concepts to each other by making a “judgment.” We judge that “all men arc mortal” or that “apples arc fruits” or that “apples are vegetables.” (False judgments arc judg- ments 100.) The mind's act of judgment. expressed in the logical structure called a “proposition” and in the linguistic structure called a “declarative sentence,” always has two parts, for it relates to each other two concepts which are expressed in two terms and two words or phrases. Onc is the “subject” and the other1s the “predicate.”.",
