          "role": "prose",
          "en": "The subject 1s what we arc talking about. The predicate 1s what we say about it.\n\nIt is easy to identify the subject and predicate of a proposition if you know grammar. For the logical subject and predicate arc always the same as the gram- matical subject and predicate.\n\nThe subject and predicate of a proposition arc not interchangeable, like the two parts of an equation on the two sides of the cqual sign. *2+2=4\" mcans the same thing as “4=2+2\"; but “God is love” docs not mean the same thing as “Love is God.” There 1s no subject or predicate in an cquation, but there is always a subject and a predicate in a proposition, and they perform different functions.\n\nTo sce this. remember that a declarative sentence is an act of communi- cation between two people, the speaker (or writer) and the listener (or read- er). What the speaker says to the listener in a proposition is this: | am going to say something about subject S. | assume you already know something about S; that you know what S means. S is old. S is behind us. And now I am gong on to something new, | will say something new about S.\n\n1 will predi- cate (affirm or deny) P of S. Perhaps you did not knowbefore that S is P. In",
