          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 152 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Linguistic expressions:\n\nI. Less than sentences (terms) 11. Sentences A. Declarative sentences (propositions) 1. Simple (categoncal) (a) Universal affirmative (b) Universal negative (¢) Particular affirmative (d) Particular negative 2. Compound (a) Hypothetical (b) Disjunctive (¢) Conjunctive B. Non-declarative sentences |. imperative 2. interrogative 3. exclamatory 4. performative 111. More than sentences (arguments) There arc other possible divisions of propositions. The most important divi- sion ofall is the division into true and false. Each kind of proposition above can be either true or false. We may not knowwhether a given propositionis true or false, but every proposition must be cither true or false. Either there are exactly 91.199 craters on the moon larger than Mcteor Crater in Arizona. or not. Either the Chicago Cubs are under a curse, and will never win a World Scrics until the end ofthe world, or not.\n\nBecause we sometimes do and sometimes do not know whether a proposi- tion is truc or false. we can also divide propositions into three kinds. (a) true (ic.\n\nknown to be true), (b) false (i.e. known to be false). and (¢) unknown. This divi- sion violates one of the rules of logical division, since it simultancously uses two standards: truc vs. false and known vs. unknown. (Sce p. 64, rule 3.) However, in dealing with the Square of Opposition (Chapter VII, Section 2) the classifi- cation of propositions into true, false. and unknown. which results from doing two different divisions at once, is very uscful and time-saving.\n\nArnstotelian vs. Modem Logic on What Propositions Do What, exactly, does a proposition do? It affirms or denies one term (the",
