          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 154 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Modem logic obscures this distinction because it reduces validin: to truth.\n\nIt interprets a syllogism like “all A 1s B and all B is C, therefore all A 1s C” as simply the claim that the following proposition is true: “All cases of proposition p (that all A is B) being true together with proposition q (that all B is C) being true are also cases of proposition ¢ (that all A is C) being true.” Truth does not reside in any other logical entitics than propositions, but it does reside clsewhere than in propositions. For example, we use the word “true” to refer to a quality that resides in some things, insofar as they conform to a stan- dard by which the mind judges them. We say “This is true money. but that 1s counterfeit.” “This is a true (onginal) Van Gogh, but that a copy.” “The publican had true picty. but the Phanisee had falsc picty.” “Hc is true to his promises.” “What the prophet says will come true.” This kind of truth — the truth in things, ontological truth — is the conformity of the thing to the mind, to an idea or design or standard in a mind, human or divine; whereas logical truth, i.e. propositional truth, as we shall sce, is the conformity of the mind to the thing.\n\nAccording to religious Judaism, Chnstianity, and Islam, the ultimate “house of truth” is the mind of God. God invents the truth of the universe, as His art; man discovers it, as his science. Human science is the indirect reading of the divine mind.\n\nWe also use the word “truce” to refer to a quality that resides in persons, insofar as they arc authentic, reliable, and honest. “He's a true man; she’s a true woman.” “True” here means “true to his word, trustable, faithful.” (There is a distinctive Hebrew word for this: emerh.) God is called “faithful and true” (emeth) in the Hebrew scriptures.\n\nOnce we have our answer to wheretruth is, it becomes easier to answer our sccond question, what truth is, especially if we confine our answer to logical truth. Anstotle defined truth, and what everyone does in fact mean by truth, in the most simple and commonscnsical possible way when he said, “If a man says of whatis that it is, or of what is not that it is not, he spcaks the truth, but if he says of what is not that it is, or of what is that it is not, he does not speak the *Y ae",
