          "en": "In the second case, | insert a *not™before “all lawyers are crooks.” “Not all lawyers are crooks™means “It's not true that all lawyers are crooks.” If I can find some lawyers who are not crooks. | have disproved the idea that all lawyers are crooks. But instcad of saying *‘some lawyers arc not crooks,” I express the very same meaning in these different words: | say, “Not all lawyers are crooks.” This is really an O proposition, and we can call it the “mcky O” proposition. It is an O that looks a bit like an E.\n\nThe third case is even trickier, because it looks even more like an E. and can be called the “very tricky O.” When 1 accent the “all” in “all lawyers are not crooks,” I mcan the same thing as in the two cases above. The meaning is an O.\n\nSo we need one totally unambiguous logical form for the E proposition, and “all S is not P** is not unambiguous (since it may mean cither the E or the “very tricky O\"), so we use the unambiguous “no S is P” instead.\n\nThe logical form for an O proposition is “some S is not P.” and this can also be ambiguous if we accent the word “some.” thereby implying that “some and only some S is not P”\" We do not usc accents, or anything ambiguous,in logical form. In logicalform, “some does not mean “only some,” or “some but not all’",
