          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 183 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Or, as you pitch in to help cook a Thanksgiving turkey, someone in the kitchen tells you, “No. no: for ‘too many cooks spoil the broth.” But someone clsc says, “Yes, yes, for ‘many hands make light work.™ Or, as you wonder whether or not to rush into something new, one person advises you, “Look before you leap.” while another says, “Strike while the iron is hot,” and **He who hesitates is lost.” Proverbs often seem to contradict each other when they do not ifthey refer to different situations. They are not as universal as they seem, and two non-uni- versal propositions do not contradict cach other. Out of sight, out of mind” is truc of weak relationships, “absence makes the heart grow fonder” is true of strong oncs. “Too many cooks spoil the broth” is true in skilled work, but ‘many hands make light work™is true in unskilled work.\n\nThe reason these proverbs do not contradict cach other is that they do not cover the same territory, so to spcak; each onc is speaking about a different thing or a different sct of things or a different situation. If one person says “many lawyers arc crooks™and the other says “many lawyers are not crooks,” they do not contradict cach other, because both subclasses of lawyers exist. But if onc says “all lawyers are crooks™and the other says, ‘no, at lcast some lawyers are not crooks,” thosc two propositions contradict cach other.",
