          "en": "VII.\n\nCONTRADICTION have refuted him if you show that “some lawyers are crooks.” You do not need to show that “All lawyers are crooks.” The relation between two opposed particular propositions, I and O,1s called subcontrariety, and the propositions are called subcontraries.\n\nSubcontrarics can both be true. This is why proverbs that seem to contradict each other often do not: because their meaning is often particular rather than universal. Two par- ticulars do not contradict cach other.\n\nSubcontraries can both be true, but subcontraries cannot both be false. If “Some lawyers are crooks” is true, it may be that “some lawyers are not crooks” is also true, and vice versa. But if “some lawyers are crooks”is false, then “some lawyers arc not crooks” cannot also be false. For if “some lawyers arc crooks” 1s false, this can be only because no lawyers are crooks. If an | is false (c.g.\n\n“Some lawyers arc crooks”), then its contradictory E must be true: “No lawyers are crooks.” The only thing that would make it false that cven some lawyers are crooks is that no lawyers at all are crooks. So if an | is false, its opposed E (its contradictory) must be true. (Remember. with contradictornies, if onc is false, the other must be true, and if one is true, the other must be false.) But if the E is true — if it is true that “No lawyers arc crooks’ — then it is certainly true that at least some lawyers are not crooks, and that is the O.",
