          "en": "Tricky propositions on the Square But no modem logic text considers denying its presupposition that | and O propositions always have existential import and A and E propositions never do, since all symbolic logic texts follow the interpretation of George Boole, the English mathematician who is onc of the founders of modem symbolic logic:\n\n“In the Boolean interpretation, universal propositions are interpreted as having no existential import, while particular propositions are not interpreted in this way.” (Copi, op. cit., p. 201) Yet clearly, in ordinary language, particular propositions sometimes do and sometimes do not have existential import. Just as “some™sometimes, in some contexts and tones of voice, implies “some but not all,” while at other times it does not,so the initial word “some™in an | or O proposition sometimes, in some contexts. implics “some of these really existing things™while at other times it does not. “Some of our ballplayers are sick™does imply that our ballplayers exist, but “Some elves are heroic™does not imply that elves exist.\n\nThe same is true for universal propositions:\n\nsometimes there is an implica- tion of existential import, sometimes not, and sometimes there is neither impli- cation. Whoever says “All of our ballplayers are sick™implies real ballplayers, but whoever says “All fictions are subjective” implies that fictions are fictional!\n\nAnd there is a third case. which makes neither of the above implications: “All nuclear terrorists who hold the whole world hostage will be instantly executed” docs not imply that there arc or ever will be any such beings — nor does it imply that there are not.",
