      "summaryEn": "Socratic Logic Page 201 covering Howto detect arguments.",
      "summaryAm": "ሶክራታዊ ሎጂክ ገጽ 201 — Howto detect arguments።",
      "panels": [
        {
          "id": "p201-1",
          "speaker": "Page 201: Howto detect arguments",
          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 201፦ Howto detect arguments",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Howto detect arguments 191] (2) be clear about what the argumentis saying, and we do this by putting it into logical form, especially the form of a syllogism. That corresponds to the net.\n\n(Advanced fishermen might do without a net, and advanced logicians can bypass the step of putting ordinary-language arguments into logical form, but beginners in logic definitely need to put an argument into logical form before they can sce whether it is valid or not, just as beginning crabbers need to get the crab in the net before they can see whether it is edible or not.) But even before we can do this (2), we must first (1) detect the presence of an argument, as we detect the presence of a crab. If we wave our net of syllogism randomly, we will probably not find an argumentin it. For there is much more in human linguistic communication than arguments, just as there is much more in the sea than crabs.\n\nDetecting the presence of an argument 1s not something that can be taught mechanically. It is intuitive “seeing.” There are mechanical principles for testing the validity of an argument, and we will lean these shortly: but there are no mechanical principles for testing for the presence of an argument.\n\nHowever, there are indicators. Oneis the presence of the essential structure of an argument, and anotheris certain key words.",
          "am": "ይህ ክፍል በገጽ 201 ላይ የቀረበውን የሶክራታዊ ሎጂክ ዋና ሐሳብ ያካትታል። ሙሉው የእንግሊዝኛ ይዘት በትይዩ ተቀምጧል።",
          "notes": "Peter Kreeft, Socratic Logic (Ed. 3.1), Page 201."
        },
        {
          "id": "p201-2",
          "speaker": "Page 201 (Continued)",
          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 201 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "(1) The essential structure of every argument consists of a relationship between its two parts: premises and conclusion. The conclusion is what you are trying to prove: the premises are your reasons, your evidence. your proof. The relationship between them can be put in different ways: we can say that the con- clusion “follows from’ the premiscs. or that the premises “entail” the conclu- sion, or ‘prove’ the conclusion, or that the premises are the “reasons” for the conclusion, or that the conclusion is true because the premises are true, or that (in a deductive argument but not an inductive one) if the premises are true, then the conclusion must be truce.\n\nIn “All men arc mortal and Socrates is a man, therefore Socrates is mortal.” the conclusion is “Socrates is mortal” because that is what you are trying to prove; and the premises are “All men are mortal” and “Socrates is a man” because that is your reason for believing that Socrates is mortal, that is your proof that Socrates is mortal, that is your evidence that Socrates is mortal. The word “therefore” asserts your claim that the premises have this logical relation to the conclusion, that they prove the conclusion to be true.\n\n(2) There is usually a key word indicating this premise-to-conclusion rela- tion. “Therefore™is the formal, proper word. but there are many others. The fol- lowing is a list of “conclusion indicators.” The proposition that follows these words is usually the conclusion of an argument; and the proposition that pre- cedes these words is usually a premise (thus argument-indicator words usually indicate hoth the conclusion and a premise):\n\n“therefore™ *hence™ “it follows that\"",
          "am": "የገጽ 201 ሁለተኛ ክፍል ውይይት እና ማብራሪያ።",
          "notes": "Peter Kreeft, Socratic Logic (Ed. 3.1), Page 201 (Part 2)."
        }
      ]
