          "role": "prose",
          "en": "(1) the physical relation between cause and effect (2) the logical relation between premise and conclusion (3) the psychological relation between motive and act (1) The relation between cause and effect is easily understood. When we say “I will dic because of cancer.” we point to death as the cffect and cancer as the causc. When we say “The Red Sox did not win a World Scrics for 86 ycars because of the Curse of the Bambino,” we point to their 86-year drought as the effect and the Curse as the cause. (More prosaic minds can substitute weak pitching as the cause.) (2) The relation between premise and conclusion is different. It is a logical relation, not a physical (#1) or psychological (#3) relation. When we say “1 will die because all men die and | am a man,” | point to the truth of **1 will di¢™as the conclusion that is proved. and 1 point to “all men die and | am a man”as the two premises which together prove that conclusion. When we say “Since the Red Sox finally defeated the Yankees in the playoffs, God must have converted and is not a Yankee fan any more.” we point to God's conversion as our conclusion and the Red Sox victory as our reason for believing that conclusion. When we say “He believes in Heaven because there has to be perfect justice in the end.” we point to his conclusion that Heaven exists and to the fact (or claim) that there must be perfect justice in the end as his premise or logical reason for believing it.\n\n(3) “Because” can also indicate a psychological motive for a mental or physical act. When | say “I think | will dic today because I am feeling despair,”",
