          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Simple argument maps map, in which the movement from premises to conclusion is symbolized by an arrow, with the conclusion at the point of the arrow and a premise at the feath- ercd end of the arrow. Two premises joined together in a syllogism look like a marriage in a genealogical table. For instance, take the classic All men are montal.\n\nSocrates 1s a man.\n\nTherefore Socrates is mortal.\n\nThe argument map for this argument would be the following:\n\n| All men are mortal TT Socrates is a man || Socrates is mortal | The horizontal line between the two premises is like a marmage: only when con- joined together do these two premises produce the legitimate child. their con- clusion.\n\nAt some point in the future it may be useful to save time by the abbreviation of using symbols for wholc propositions instcad of writing them out; but at this point, to avoid any possibility of confusion. you should take the time to patient- ly write out the whole proposition in each box when using argument maps.\n\nTake the following linear argument:\n\nAll that is matenal is extended in space.\n\nAnd all that is extended in space can be divided.",
          "am": "ይህ ክፍል በገጽ 217 ላይ የቀረበውን የሶክራታዊ ሎጂክ ዋና ሐሳብ ያካትታል። ሙሉው የእንግሊዝኛ ይዘት በትይዩ ተቀምጧል።",
