          "role": "prose",
          "en": "IX. DIFFERENT KINDS OF ARGUMENTS Now take the following cumulative argument: “There must be a life after death, because life would be unendurable otherwise, and because nearly cvery culture in history teaches it, and because there has to be a final judgment, and also because we're not just bodies but spints too.” Here. four different premises all are brought up to prove the same conclu- sion. The argument map for this argument would be:\n\nLitc would not be |[Nearly every culturc|| There has to be|| We're not just ndurable otherwise [fin history teaches at|| a final judgment| bodies but spints ams peSee | There 1s lite after death ] There 1s no honzontal line between the premises because cach one is “unmarned.” so to speak: independent.\n\nIn the next few chapters we will learn how to judge how strong cach of these arguments is. An argument map does not evaluate the strength of an argument, but only diagrams its strategy. The strength of an argument depends on three things: whether the terms are clear, whether the premises are true, and whether the logical progressions are logically valid. We have already studied terms and the technique for making them clear: definition. We will soon study the rules for judging whether any given argumentis logically valid or invalid. But there is no onc technique or sct of rules for judging whether any given proposition is true or false.",
          "am": "ይህ ክፍል በገጽ 218 ላይ የቀረበውን የሶክራታዊ ሎጂክ ዋና ሐሳብ ያካትታል። ሙሉው የእንግሊዝኛ ይዘት በትይዩ ተቀምጧል።",
          "notes": "Peter Kreeft, Socratic Logic (Ed. 3.1), Page 218."
