          "speaker": "Page 224 (Continued)",
          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 224 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "(2) Skepticism denies that we can go beyond Step | and have any reliable knowl- edge even in Step 2, sense perception. (3) Radical empiricism denies that we can go beyond Step 2. (4) Moderate Empincism denies that we can go beyond Step 3. reliable but only probable generalizations from sense experience. (5) Extreme Rationalism claims that Step 4, understanding essences.\n\nis innate rather than dependent on expenience. (6) Rationalism claims that Step 5, deductive reason- ing, can be certain without depending on Step 2, sense perception. And (7) Kant- 1an Idealism gets all the parts together but orders them wrongly. working the cir- cle backwards. imposing categones (essences) on experience rather than denv- ing them from it.\n\nThe reason why Socrates’ argument in Book 1 of the Republic is not satis- fying to him (scc the last paragraph of Book I), and why it should not be satis- fying to us, is that Step 4 is missing (an understanding of the essential nature of justice); and this is supplied by the rest of the Republic. The argument in Book I is formally valid (and, by the way. very complex; an argument map of all its steps would look like an archery contest), and its obscrvational data basc and its inductive reasoning (Steps 2 and 3) are sound. But that is all. This example (the difference between Book | and Books [1-X of the Republic) illustrates the infe- nority of modern symbolic logic to classical Socratic-Aristotelian logic. Correct manipulation of symbols according to computer-like rules of consistency, plus cmpincal evidence, is not enough to convince human beings of a truth, even if expressed in all the complex and sophisticated techniques of symbolic logic, because human beings are “rational,” and “rational” means not merely calculai- ing but understanding; not merely the third act of the mind but the first; not merely the fifth step of the Socratic Mcthod but the fourth. That is why Plato put this step at the rop of his “divided line™in Book 6 of the Republic, which sum- marizes the steps of the education of the philosopher.",
