          "role": "prose",
          "en": "And humans are not machincs.\n\nTherefore humans are not computers.\n\n(3) What if S and P arc both related to M negatively? Then we knownoth- ing about how they are related to cach other. We cannot prove a negative con- clusion from two negative premises. For instance, No men are insects.\n\nAnd no insects write logic books.\n\nTherefore no men wnte logic books.\n\nis obviously an invalid argument. Its premises are both true, but its conclusion is false.\n\nImagine S, M, and P as warring nations, which can be cither allics or ene- mics. (In affirmative propositions the two terms are like allies; in negative propositions they are like enemies.) Let us look at three possible cases: (1) no negative premises, (2) one negative premise, and (3) two negative premiscs, (1) If S and M are allies, and M and P are allics, then S and P must be allies.\n\n(E.g. if France and England are allies, and England and America are allies, then France and America must be allies.) (2A) If S and M are enemies, and M and P are allies, then S and P must be encmies. (E.g. if America and Germany are enemies, and Germany and Italy are allies, then Amcrica and ltaly must be encmics.) (2B) If S and M are allies, and M and P are enemies, then S and P must be cnemics. (E.g. if England and America are allies, and America and Japan are cnemics, then England and Japan must be encmics.) (3) If S and M are cnemies, and M and P are enemics, then S and P maybe allies or cnemies. (E.g. if Amenca and Germany are enemies, and Germany and Russia are enemies. then Amcrica and Russia may be either allies or encmies.) The saying is not always truc that “the enemy of my encmy is my friend.” But the ally of my encmy must be my enemy.",
