          "role": "prose",
          "en": "“Sis P” is not like **2+2=4\", since P can reveal five different aspects of the comprehension of S. But modern symbolic logic has no doctrine of the predica- bles. It ignores the comprehension of a term, for this means its nature or essence, and this notion conflicts with nominalism.\n\nIt also ignores the distinctively human power to comprehend a term's comprehension, and uses only those data- processing and calculating powers of the mind that we share with a computer, thus in practice reducing the human mind to a computer, “natural intelligence” to “artificial intelligence.” There is, thus, an important human and philosophical issue at the heart of the divide between classical and modern logic. Whether this last sentence is true or not is precisely one of the issues that divide the two log- ics. But modern logic cannot help us argue about that issuc! A merely formal, algebraic, computer-language logic cannot deal with the issues about the nature of man and knowledge that arc part and parcel of the very essence of philoso- phy, because it cannot deal with essences, natures, universals.\n\nMill intended his dilemma against syllogism to show the superionty of induction to deduction; but in fact it is equally fatal against induction. For an inductive argument which draws a universal conclusion from particular instances is also subject to the same dilemma of “tautology or non sequitur.” If it is a complete induction,i.e. if the premises cover all instances of the univer- sal conclusion, then it is a tautology: if not,it is a non sequitur. “John, Jim, Joe, and Jeb arc cach members of the Issaquah Barbershop Quartet, and cach one is male, therefore all members of the 1.B.Q. are male”is a tautology (since a quar- tet by definition has only four members). But “John and Jim are members ofthe 1.B.Q. and cach is male, therefore all members of the 1.B.Q. are male” is a non",
