          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Venn diagrams are (1) always rchable, and (2) fairly easy to learn; howev- er, they (3) do not show whyany syllogism is valid or invalid. It is not absolute- ly necessary to learn Venn diagrams, since Arnstotle’s six rules do all that Venn diagrams do and more; but they are a useful second way of checking.\n\n“Barbara Celarent” is the first line of a medieval list of artificial names which is a clever and charming but complicated and cumbersome way ofjudg- ing the validity of a syllogism by its structure of mood and figure. (This will be explained shortly.) It is (1) universally reliable, but (2) the most difficult of all to usc, and (3) it docs not show why any syllogism is valid or invalid. This his- torical curiosity will be explained briefly. but it is an unnecessary extra, useful mainly for mental exercise.\n\nSection |. Euler’s Circles (B) Not only can most syllogisms be checked for validity by Euler's Circles, but the strategy of the syllogism can bc scen most clearly by this method. However, it will not give a clear result for some syllogisms with | or O premises (perhaps 5-10% ofthe syllogisms you will meet).\n\nIf your memory is a little dim, you should first revicw the four Euler dia- grams for the A, E, I and O propositions. (Sce page 152.) The technique for evaluating syllogisms by Euler's Circles 1s very simple:",
