          "en": "XI. CHECKING SYLLOGISMS FOR VALIDITY just diagram borh premises by Euler diagrams, supenmposing one on the other.\n\nThis is possible because there is always a common term to any two propositions in a syllogism. A syllogism has only three terms, remember; if there are more than three, we have cither no syllogism at all, or a syllogism with “the Fallacy of Four Terms,” or perhaps two syllogisms in a chain. (We will soon learn howto detect these patterns.) Take the easiest casc first, our old friend “All men are mortal, and Socrates 1s a man, thercfore Socrates is mortal.” We first diagram “all men are mortal™in Euler circles:\n\nmortal Then we supenmpose the diagram for the second premise.\n\n“Socrates is a man,” on our diagram for the first premise, like a double exposure in photogra- phy. thus showing how the two premises look together. The circle for “man”is already there, so all we have to do is to include the term “Socrates” in it. Forin an A proposition (such as “Socrates 1s mortal”), the subject is included in the extension of the predicate, as a sub-population is included in a population. (In terms of comprehension, on the other hand, the predicate 1s part of the (meaning of the) subject).\n\n(Sora man mortal Now we simply look to see whether we necessarily have the conclusion ir the diagram of the premises. For the conclusion does not add any newdata to the premises. The premises by themselves supply all the data, or information; and the conclusion only reveals the truth that is already implicit in the premises.",
