          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 258 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Compassion is a virtue.\n\nJustice is not compassion.\n\nTherefore justice 1s not a virtue.\n\nLet's first test this syllogism by Euler circles, and then by the six rules. The Euler diagram shows how both premises can be truc without the conclusion being truc, for there are two possibilities:\n\njustice And Euler circles must be drawn for all possibilitics to see whether there is any possibility that the premises can be true without the conclusion being true. (For this reason, it is sometimes casier to use the six rules than to use Euler circles.) The six rules reveal the fallacy here as “illicit major”: the major term, “virtue,” moves from being undistributed in the major premise to being distrib- uted in the conclusion. The predicate of the conclusion is the major term. This",
