          "en": "X1. CHECKING SYLLOGISMS FOR VALIDITY Corollary 1 forbids any syllogism to have two particular premises. Any syl- logism that violates this rule will also violate Rule 3 or 4. For example:\n\nSome swans are white.\n\nSome jewels arc green.\n\nAnd some white things are beautiful.\n\nAnd some green things arc alive.\n\nTherefore some swans are beautiful.\n\nTherefore some jewels arc alive.\n\nHerc again we have the same logical form, and both syllogisms contain two true premises, but only the first has a truc conclusion. The conclusion is truc “by accident,” so to speak; it does not necessarily follow from the premises. The fal- lacy is Undistributed Middle. Or consider the following:\n\nSome onions are not smelly.\n\nSome sheep are not black.\n\nAnd some smelly things taste good.\n\nAnd some black things arc animals.",
