          "en": "2358 XI. CHECKING SYLLOGISMS FOR VALIDITY major premise first. But when using “Barbara Celarent™it is, for the mood of a syllogism is always expressed in a three-letter summary that is in this order: first the major premise, then the minor premise, then the conclusion.\n\nThe figure of a syllogism is the placement of the middle term. There arc four possibilities: the middle term can be (1) the subject of the major premisc and the predicate of the minor premise (= the “first figurc™) (2) the predicate of both premises (= the “second figure™) (3) the subject of both premises (= the “third figure™) (4) the predicate of the major premise and the subject of the minor premise (= the “fourth figure”) M—P P—M M—P P—M S—M S—M M—S M—S First Second Third Fourth Figure Figure Figure Figure “Barbara Celarent”™1s a mnemonic device to remember the valid moods for cach figure. The vowels of the names make up the mood (c.g. “Barbara” = AAA) and there are four lines of names for the four figures:\n\nBarbara, Celarent, Darii, Ferio (AAA, EAE, All EIQ) Camestres, Cesare, Baroko, Festino (AEE, EAE, AOO, E10) Darapti. Disamis, Datisi, Fclapton, Bokardo, Ferison (AAl IAL All, EAQ, 0OAO, EIO) Bramantip, Camenes, Dimaris, Fesapo, Fresison (AAl, AEE, IAL EAQ, E10) The medievals loved these lines, for they were a key (though a slow one) that opencd up all valid syllogisms and locked away all invalid oncs. They also knew and used Aristotle’s six rules, but the Latin verses seemed more clegant, almost a magical formula or incantation. Tastes change; the laws of logic do not.",
