          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 273 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Therefore some green things are clever.\n\nThis violates none ofAristotle's six rules because if we interpret all propo- sitions as cxistentially ncutral, the validity ofthis syllogism does not depend on whether leprechauns exist or not. The term “green things” in the first premise (“all leprechauns are green™) is no more nor less existential than the same term “green things™in the conclusion (‘some green things are clever”).\n\nThere arc three reasons we do not insist on Venn diagrams for beginners:\n\nour preference for the Aristotelian over the Boolean interpretation of proposi- tions; the fact that both Anistotle’s six rules and Euler’s circles reveal more sim- ply and clearly whya syllogism is fallacious; and the fact that Aristotle's rules (and certainly Euler's circles) are simpler and easier to use. Anistotle’s six rules may not be casier to learn than Venn diagrams, but once learned they are easicr and quicker to use than Venn diagrams. You will eventually be able to recognize violations of the six rulcs at a glance, even in ordinary language, without trans- lating into strict logical form, while this is not true of Venn diagrams.",
