          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 274፦ XII. More Difficult Syllogisms",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "XII. More Difficult Syllogisms Section 1. Enthymemes: abbreviated syllogisms (B) An cnthymeme is a syllogism with one of its three propositions implied but not stated. The word *“enthymeme” comes from the Greek “en-thymos™which means “in mind.” since onc of the three propositions is “kept in mind” rather than being explicitly stated in writing or speech.\n\nThis is a time-saving device. an abbreviation; and we all do it all the time.\n\nThe fact that we all use enthymemes in ordinary speech shows that we all have a working knowledge of the principles of syllogistic logic in our unconscious minds. A book like this onc merely brings our unconscious knowledge to con- sciousness, like psychoanalysis.\n\n“The search for the tacit premise is excellent intellectual training .",
          "am": "ይህ ክፍል በገጽ 274 ላይ የቀረበውን የሶክራታዊ ሎጂክ ዋና ሐሳብ ያካትታል። ሙሉው የእንግሊዝኛ ይዘት በትይዩ ተቀምጧል።",
