          "role": "prose",
          "en": "(It 1s usually stated. Third order enthymemcs arc fairly rare, and usually obvious.) In our example, the conclu- sion is: “Teenagers can't buy beer in this state.” (3) Put it into logical form: “No [teenagers] are [those who can buy beer in this statc].” (4) Identify the expressed premise; “They are minors.” (5) Putit into logical form: “All [teenagers] arc [minors].” (Translating the pronoun “they” into its noun, “tecnagers.”) (6) If you already have a common term, proceed to step (7). If you do not have an explicit common term (i.e. a word or phrasc used once in cach of the two propositions without any word change), and yet you scnse that there is an argument here (i.c. a connection between the premise and the conclusion such that the premise is some sort of reason for the conclusion), then try rewording onc ofthe propositions to conform to the other one, thus getting an explicit com- mon term. The basic rule is that you may change the wording but not the mean- ing. Herc are some of the most common wording changes:\n\n(6a) All pronouns should be changed to the nouns they refer to.\n\n(6b) If you have two words or phrases that mean exactly the same thing, they are synonyms. Synonyms should be changed into cach other. E.g. in “Holy people aren't gloomy: that's why Stoics aren't saints,” “saints” and “holy pcople™ are synonyms.\n\n(6¢) Bord order can be changed, e.g. passive voice and active voice can be interchanged. E.g. “1 love you, therefore you cannot die” can be changed to **You are loved by me, therefore you cannot dic,” thus giving us the common term “you.” (6d) Possessives can be reworded as independent nouns. E.g. “Since he's a doctor, his bag probably contains drugs” can be changed to “He is a doctor, thercfore he is one whose bag probably contains drugs.” (6¢) If a short term appears alone as a complete term in one proposition and reappears as part of a longer term in another proposition. change the wording of",
