          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 278 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "(7c) Now that you have the mood of the missing premise, write out the “empty” logicalform for that mood, with brackets around the yct-to-be-supplicd subject and predicate terms. In our cxample, the missing premise is an E, so the empty logical forms: No []is[ ].\n\n(7d) Next, find the nvo terms that arc to be joined in this missing premise, They are the two terms that have been used only once. One of them will be the middlc term. which you can identify at a glance by the fact that it 1s the only term not in the conclusion.\n\nIn our example, the two terms to be joined in the missing premise are “minors” and “those who can legally buy beer in this state.” (7¢) Once you have the two terms, the only decision remaining is which of these two terms will be the subject and which the predicate. Try both arrange- ments, and if onc of them gives you a fallacy, drop it and use the other.\n\nDo not think:\n\n[| will use whatever order of terms gives me a true premise; for there can be dispute about whatis true, but there is no question about what is fallacious. You want to supply whatever missing premise will make the syllo- gism valid. For even if you are arguing against an opponent, you want to be fair",
