          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Next, we need to change the predicate (*'shall inhent the carth™) to a noun phrase (“those who shall inhent the earth.”) Now we have the expressed premise in logical form: “All [the meek] are [those who shall inherit the earth).” We have a common term between the two expressed propositions.\n\nIt is “the meek.” So we do not have the problem of finding or making a common term.\n\nThe next step is to write down the logical form the missing premise has to have. Is it A, E. I, or O? Since both of the two expressed premises are A, the missing premise will have to be A to avoid violating rule 5 or 6. So it will be the most typical form of syllogism, AAA, a “bull's eye syllogism.” So we write out the logical form for the implied A: “all [ Jare [ ].” Now we need to find the two terms. How? Look at the terms in the two propositions we have already written out in logical form. Remember the struc- turc of a syllogism: Each term must be used twice. And “the meek™has already been used twice, while “those who are blessed” and “those who shall inhent the earth” have both been used only once. So cach of these two terms must be used onc more time, by being joined in the missing premise.\n\nNext, we need to find what order to put them in. There are two possibilities:\n\n“All [those who shall inherit the earth] are [those who are blessed],” or “All [those who are blessed) are [those who shall inhent the carth].” Which is it?\n\nCommon sense might click in at this point and tell us the answer; but sup- pose it docs not. In that case. let us try both possibilities. and see which one gives us a valid syllogism.",
