          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Enthymemes: abbreviated syllogisms If we try as our missing premise “all {those who shall inherit the earth] are [those who arc blessed],we have the following syllogism:\n\nAll (those who shall inherit the carth] are [those who are blessed) And all [the meek] are [those who shall inherit the carth] Therefore all [the meek] are [those who are blessed).\n\nThis is a perfect bull's eye syllogism, and valid. But if we had used the other possibility for our missing premise, namely “All [those who are blessed] are [those who shall inhenit the earth),” we would have had:\n\nAll [those who are blessed] are [those who shall inhent the earth] All [the meck] are [those who shall inhent the carth] Therefore all [the meek] are [those who are blessed) And this gives us Undistributed Middle.\n\nNow there is absolutely no rcason to saddle the speaker with this missing premise (“all the blessed shall inherit the earth™), which would make his argu- ment fallacious, since he did not commit himself to this premise. Clearly, he is implying the other premise, the onc that would make his argument valid. So that is the missing premise (“all who shall inhent the earth are blessed™).\n\nRemember, if you want to criticize another person’s enthymeme, it is not fair to saddle him with a premise that would make his argument invalid if there is another premisc that would not. Even if you think the premise that makes his argument invalid is true, and the premise that makes his argument valid is falsc, not everyone will agree as to which propositions are truce and which are false, but everyone will agree as to which arguments are valid and which are invalid.",
