          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 287 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Therefore he who drinks, goes to Heaven.\n\nObviously there is some ambiguity operating in the terms here. (Where?\n\nFind it.) But formally the argument is valid and looks like this in Euler's circles:\n\nhe who docs not sin he who goes to Heaven Sometimes the terms need a little rewording to go into logical form. E.g.:\n\n“Since happiness consists in peace of mind, and since durable peace of mind depends on the confidence we have in the future, and since that confidence 1s based on the science we should have of the nature of God and the soul, it fol- lows that that science is necessary for true happiness” (Leibniz).\n\nPutting the propositions into more logical form, this becomes:\n\n(1) All happiness is peace of mind (2) All pcace of mind depends on the confidence we have in the future (3) Whatever depends on the confidence we have in the future, depends on that whichis based on the science of God and the soul (4) Therefore all happiness depends on that which is based on the science of God and the soul.",
