          "en": "Hypothetical syllogisms (3) But suppose we follow “If the wind blows, the paper falls™with “The paper falls” in the second premise, and then draw the conclusion that “The wind blows.” This is invalid because the conclusion does not necessarily follow. Just because the paper falls, it does not mean the wind blows; another cause could producc the same cffect, such as a person blowing it off, or flipping it off- or even an carthquake. “Affirming the Consequent™is an invalid form:\n\nP=>9 q © p (4) And “Denying the Antecedent™in the second premise is also invalid. ~If the wind blows. the paper falls, and the wind does not blow, therefore the paper does not fall” forgets all those other possible causes of the paper falling that were mentioned above.\n\nP=>9 ~p so ~q Both fallacies make the same mistake: they begin with p © q and then pro- ceed as if it were q@ OD p. We can no more “convert” a hypothetical proposition than we can validly convert a simple A proposition. “Whenever the wind blows, the paper falls™docs not say the same thing as “Whenever the paper falls, the wind blows.” “All cascs of A are cases of B™does not say the same thing as “all cascs of B are cases of A.” A hypothetical syllogism can be an enthymeme, omitting any one ofits three propositions. just as a simple syllogism can. And it can also be an epicheirema, adding a reason to one or both of its premises.\n\nThus there arc three valid forms of hypothetical syllogisms and two falla- cious forms. The vahd forms are:",
