          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Key: p = we do mean something by such words Symbolization:\n\n~p > ~q q = We use such words consistently q © p Section 3. The practical syllogism:\n\narguing about means and ends There are three reasons we scek knowledge by reason, Anstotle noted: (1) simply to know, for its own sake; (2) to knowfor thc sake of doing somcthing, for some action or practice; or (3) to knowfor the sake of making something, changing something, or repainng something. The first he called *“‘thcoretical™ reasoning: the second, “practical” reasoning; and the third “productive” reason- ing, reasoning directed toward what we now call “technology.” These three motives for reasoning produce the three kinds of “sciences™that he distin- guished: “theoretical sciences,” practical sciences,” and “productive sciences.” Reasoning with a purely theoretical (or “speculative™or “‘contemplative™) end comes in many different forms, and we have been exploring these different forms throughout this book. Practical reasoning, however, is much simpler: its form is usually a pure or mixed hypothetical syllogism. E.g.:\n\nIf I am to get to England by tomorrow, | must take a plane.\n\nIf | take a plane. | must buy a ticket now, Therefore if | am to get to England by tomorrow, | must buy a ticket now.\n\nOr simply:",
