          "role": "prose",
          "en": "The practical syllogism: arguing about means and ends If I am to get to England by tomorrow I must buy a ticket today.\n\nI want to get to England by tomorrow.\n\nTherefore 1 must buy a ticket today.\n\nThis is “practical” reasoning in the modem sense of the word “practical”:\n\npragmatic, instrumental, useful. But “practical” can also mean “moral,” and this moral dimension of human practice also is often expressed in the form of hypo- thetical syllogisms:\n\nIf you want to be blessed or happy or perfect, you must practice all the virtucs.\n\nYou do want to be blessed or happy or perfect.\n\nTherefore you must practice all the virtues.\n\nSome will object that this sounds too much like “the end justifies the means.” But of course it docs! What else could possibly justify a means? That's what “means” means. When we say “the end does not justify the means™we mean that a good end docs notjustify an evil means. Butit surely justifies a good means. Even if you accept Kant's famous criticism ofthis “teleological” or “end- oriented” morality and insist that we should will our moral duty rather than our happiness, that we should try to simply “do the right thing” because it’s nght, it remains true that our moral reasoning usually takes the form of a hypothetical syllogism:\n\nWe must always do the night thing.\n\nIf we tell a lie, we do not do the right thing.",
          "am": "ይህ ክፍል በገጽ 307 ላይ የቀረበውን የሶክራታዊ ሎጂክ ዋና ሐሳብ ያካትታል። ሙሉው የእንግሊዝኛ ይዘት በትይዩ ተቀምጧል።",
          "notes": "Peter Kreeft, Socratic Logic (Ed. 3.1), Page 307."
