          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 309፦ The practical syllogism: arguing about means and ends",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "The practical syllogism: arguing about means and ends Exercises on hypothetical syllogisms: Put into correct symbolic form and test for validity. If there is a fallacy, name it. Remember, there may be a few cnthymemcs or epicheiremas too. There are no practical syllogisms here.\n\n.\n\n“There is no case known (neither is it, indeed, possible) in which a thing is found to be the efficient cause of itself; for in such a case it would be prior to itself, which is impossible.” (St. Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologica 1, 2,3) “If light consisted of matenal particles, it would possess momentum. It can- not therefore consist of matenal particles.” . “If the mountain will not come to Muhammad, Muhammad will come to the mountain. But Muhammad will come to the mountain. Thercfore the moun- tain will not come to Muhammad.” “If the objective of marnage were contentment, then the discontent of either party would be a sufficient reason for annulling it.” “If there 1s such a thing as space, it must be in something. For all being is in something, and that which is in something is in space. So space will be in space. and so on ad infinitum. Accordingly, there is no such thing as space.” (Zcno the Elcatic) St. Augustine argued, against the skeptic: If you are deceived, you must exist. But you say you are deceived. therefore you can be certain you exist.",
