          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Therefore we will get wet.\n\nAll occasions ofit raining arc occasions of our getting wet.\n\nThis is an occasion of it raining.\n\nTherefore this is an occasion of our getting wet.\n\nEither it will not rain, or we will get wet.\n\nIt will not not-rain. (It will rain.) Therefore we will get wet.\n\nIt can’t be true both that it will rain and that we will not get wet.\n\nIt is true that it will rain. (It will rain.) Therefore it is not truc that we will not get wet, (We will get wet.) Exercises on Conjunctive Syllogisms: Put into symbolic form,test for validity, to and imagine howyou might reply to cach of the following. Some may be enthymemes or epicheiremas, and these may require a more complex sym- bolization. For an additional exercise, also transform cach into disjunctive, hypothetical, and categorical forms.\n\n. “You can’t be both you and somebody clse. That's why you're not somebody else.” “Vampires are ‘the undead.’ But nothing can be both dead and undead.\n\nTherefore vampires are not dcad. And nothing can be both dcad and alive.\n\nTherefore vampires are alive.” “You can’t both have your cake and eat it too. That's why you can cat your cake.” (A student's critique of Plato's attempt to write a philosophical dialogue on",
