          "en": "Dilemmas An example: Socrates, asked by a disciple whether he thought it a good idea to get married, replied Yes, because:\n\nIf you have a happy marriage, you will attain happiness.\n\nIf you have an unhappy marriage, you will attain wisdom.\n\nYou must either have a happy marriage or an unhappy one.\n\nTherefore you will attain either happiness or wisdom.\n\nThe Four Forms of Dilemmas A dilemma can be cither simple or complex, and either constructive (affir- mative) or destructive (negative). This gives us four forms: Simple Constructive, Simple Destructive, Complex Constructive, and Complex Destructive. Here are their forms:\n\nSimple Constructive:\n\n(p> q)& (r>q) Ifp,thenq; and ifr, thenq pvr Eitherporr q Therefore q Complex Constructive: (p © q) & (ros) If p, then q; and if r, then s pvr Eitherporr qVvs Therefore citherq ors Simple Destructive:",
