          "en": "(3) Formulate the two premises.\n\nConstructing a good dilemma involves three steps too; the last two are sim- ilar and the first is identical to the three above.\n\n(1) Formulate the conclusion you want to prove.\n\n(2) Find two appropriate “horns” for your dilemma, the two alternatives which both lead to your conclusion. These should ideally be mutually exclusive and jointly exhaustive statcments, X or non-x (3) Formulate the two premises. (a) The two “homs™are your double *if.\n\n. then...” premise. (b) And the disjunctive (“either . .. or. ..”) premise forces your opponent to choose between these two thorns.” For instance, suppose you want to prove that “God exists.” You might use these two “horns”: “You must be either a theist or an atheist.” Then develop the “if... then...” consequences of the two horns as follows, onc quickly and the other through a long chain of consequences: “If you are a theist, you admit that God exists. If you are an atheist, you claim to knowthat God docs not exist: and if you claim to know that God docs not exist, you claim to know that God does not exist anywhere in all reality; and if you claim to know that Goes does not exist anywhere in all reality, you claim to know all reality; and if you claim to knowall reality, you claim you arc omniscient; and if you claim you are omnis- cient, you claim you are God; and if you claim you arc God, you claim God exists. Therefore, if you are an atheist, you claim God exists.” The above argument is probably not going to convince manypeople. Butit is a clever debating device. Dilemmas are usually more *fun™than senous. The above dilemma can casily be answered by escaping between the homs and cmbracing agnosticism.",
