          "role": "prose",
          "en": "3/8 XIV. INDUCTION 5. Summarize and evaluate the point of the following passage:\n\n**l have never understood why there is supposed to be something crabbed or antique about a syllogism; still less can I understand what anybody means by talking as if induction had somchow taken the place of deduction. The whole point of deduction is that true premises produce a true conclusion.\n\nWhat is called induction seems simply to mean collecting a large number oftrue premises, or perhaps, in some physical matters, taking rather more trouble to see that they arc true. It may be a fact that a modern man can get morc out of a great many premises, concerning microbes or asteroids, than a medieval man could get out of a very few premises about salamanders and unicorns. But the process of deduction from the data is the same for the modem mind as for the medieval mind.\n\n.\n\n.\n\n. It was the misfortune of medieval culture that there were not enough true premises, owing to the rather ruder conditions of travel or expenment.\n\nBut however perfect were the conditions of travel or expenment, they could only produce premises; it would still be necessary to deduce conclusions. But many modern people talk as if what they call induction were some magic way of reaching a conclusion without using any of those hornd old syllogisms. But .",
          "am": "ይህ ክፍል በገጽ 328 ላይ የቀረበውን የሶክራታዊ ሎጂክ ዋና ሐሳብ ያካትታል። ሙሉው የእንግሊዝኛ ይዘት በትይዩ ተቀምጧል።",
