          "id": "p329-2",
          "speaker": "Page 329 (Continued)",
          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 329 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "“Explaining things by discovering their causes™was Aristotle's definition of science; and modern science, in spite of its much “tighter” method, has not essentially altered that definition.\n\nScience seeks to explain things by discover- ing their causcs (this is “pure science” or “theoretical science™), and to change things by changing their causes (this is “applied science™or “practical science’:\n\ntechnology).\n\nBut what kinds of causcs? There are at least five distinctions we need to makc among causcs.\n\n(a) We have already (page 193) distinguished (1) real causes from logical “becauses,” i.c. causes of a thing's being from causes of our knowledge of it.",
