          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 338 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "(7) What increasingly verifies the hypothesis is success in predicting and controlling what could not otherwise be predicted or controlled. At a certain point the hypothesis is venificd and becomes a scientific law. There is some dis- agreement about where that “certain point” lies. We usually distinguish a hypothesis, as possible,a theory. as probable. and a law, as venfied (proved).\n\nSection 5. Statistical probability Students will be relieved to discover that this section is very short. (Courses on statistics are usually onc of the most dreaded.) But it must be mentioned, how- ever briefly. because it constitutes onc of the main kinds of inductive argument.\n\nThe two fields that spawned probability theory, and still rely on statistics the most. are gambling and insurance. Pascal - a 17th-century gambler. mathemati- cian. and philosopher — worked out the first probability theory for gambling. (He also invented the first working computer. an adding machine.) All forms of inductive arguments arc only probable. But there arc two dif- ferent kinds of probability, or meanings of the word “probable.” and onlyonc of them is quantifiable statistically. (1) The probability of a penny coming down tails on any one toss is 50%. And the probability of living longer than 75 years in a time and place where the average life expectancy 1s 75 is slightly less than 50%. (These two examples are instances of two different kinds of quantitatively measurable probability, but we will not go into this distinction here.) (2) [tis also very probable. on present evidence, that the Big Bang happened 15 billion years ago, but we cannot quantify this probability.\n\nIt is probable that a certain build- ing will not collapse, that your teacher will not pull out a gun and shoot you, and that little green men will not land a flying saucer on the White House lawn, but we cannot quantify any ofthese probabilitics (all of which, in turn,arc also dif- ferent kinds of probability). If there arc inductive arguments for thesc conclu- sions, the arguments can be only probable, since the conclusion of an inductive argument “follows” from its premises only with probability. not certainty. Some inductive arguments can be assigned a quantitative degree of probability: others cannot.",
