          "en": "XIV. INDUCTION application to the case we are arguing about. The general principle is that jus- tice, or rightcousness,is the advantage of the weaker, and the application is that moral and political justice too must be not the advantage of the stronger but of the weaker. If Socrates had shortened and simplified this already-abbreviated argument (minus step 3) to a onc-step argument by analogy, it would consist in arguing from the cases of the doctor. teacher, and horseman to the similar case of the politician.\n\nHere is another example:\n\nI am lost in a jungle, very hungry, and have no food. I wonder which of the unfamiliar fruits I see is safe to eat. | observe mon- keys eating a certain fruit that looks a little like a mango, so | cat it too, since monkeys arc more similar to man than any other animal is. Instead of reasoning in the four steps above, | have taken a short cut and reasoned directly from what I know of mangoes (that they are safe to eat) to this mango-like fruit (that it is too), and from monkeys to men. intuiting (or hoping) that they arc similar cnough.\n\n3.",
