          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Tiffany:\n\nXIV.\n\nINDUCTION An Exercise on Nearly Everything The following argumentative dialogue tests your ability to integrate the whole of this book in a practical way: to know which logical tools are needed to cvaluate cach step of the long argument, and to use them.\n\nThe topic is deliberately controversial (feminism, gender, and women's “roles™) to test your ability to be objective and logical even about an emotional- ly volatile issue.\n\nThe numbers in the margin arc for casy reference. They correspond to dis- tinct steps in the conversation, each requinng its own logical analysis and cval- uation, as well as being related to its preceding and following steps.\n\nYou may not be able to sce howall 71 distinct steps can be explained and cvaluated by some specific point in this book, but the more you can do, the bet- ter. This would be a good exercise to work on with one or two other students, to multiply the angles of vision and to discuss cach point together.\n\nFatima is a very conservative Iraqi Muslim. Tiffany is a very “liberal” Amencan. Socrates is — well, Socrates. He enters the conversation near the end and produces some consternation in both his dialogue partners, as is his wont.\n\nFatima; Tiffany:\n\nFatima:\n\nTiffany:\n\nFatima:\n\nTiffany:\n\nFatima:\n\nTiffany:\n\nFatima:\n\nTiffany:\n\nFatima:\n\nTiffany:\n\n10 Fatima:\n\nTiffany:",
