          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 355 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "What would the real, histoncal Socrates have said here? Socrates’ method 1s only partially technical and able to be formulated objectively and impersonally. Lea both parts, but don’t neglect the inner spint.\n\n5.\n\nThere are two ways to write a Socratic dialogue: (a) You can simply use your imagination, be Socrates and his dialogue partner (let us call him “0” for “other”), and let the argument and the two personalities carry you wherever they naturally go, like a iver. (b) Or you can make a logical map of the argument before you begin, and add the dramatic and personal dimension to it as you write. If (b) proves too un-Socratic and artificial, try (a). If (a) proves to be too unstructured, use (b) at least for a while.\n\nAfter some practice with (b) you may be able to transition to (a).\n\n6.\n\nWhether you use (a) or (b) above, you must know logic, naturally and instinctively. Don't think you can master this section without mastering the rest of this book.\n\n7.\n\nIn the process of arguing, use the rules for Socratic debate on pages 348-50. Also sce pages 211-14.\n\nB.\n\nPoints of advice in constructing the dramatic character of Socrates:\n\n1.\n\nConfine yourself to only two characters, Socrates and “0.” Perhaps later you can add other characters, as Plato docs, but even then each should take",
          "am": "የገጽ 355 ሁለተኛ ክፍል ውይይት እና ማብራሪያ።",
