          "en": "The personal, psychological struggle is as much a part of the Socratic dia- logue as the struggle of ideas. A Socratic dialogue is a form of spiritual warfare, therapy. or doctoring to the spirit of the student. Yet paradoxical- ly. it is for this reason that you must avoid direct personal confrontation and let the argument always be the object of attention. Socrates sees him- self and *O™not as a winner and a loser but as two scientists mutually seeking the truth by testing two alternative hypotheses.\n\nWhichever one finds the truth, both are winners.\n\n8.\n\nSocrates’ goal is always ultimately somchow moral (though this is not always apparent at first). For he has only onc lifetime, and it is too pre- cious to waste on issues that are not somchow connected to the most important purpose of human life, becoming more wise and virtuous.\n\n9.\n\nSocrates’ aim is not to harm but to help \"0. Sometimes, this involves shame, but it ncver involves a conflict of interests — at least not from Socrates’ point of view. “O™may or may not understand this, but Socrates, likc Jesus, is altruistic in his very offensivencss. He believes, as Aquinas says, that “there 1s no greater act of chanty one can do to his neighbor than to lead him to the truth.” Socratic dialogue is ultimatcly missionary work.\n\nC. Points of advice about the logical method:\n\nI.\n\nThe question must be defined carly on.\n\n2.\n\nThe question should be formulated disjunctively, so that it has only two",
