          "role": "prose",
          "en": "How to have a Socratic debate until he has first restated that argument, in his own words (to prove that he understands the meaning, not just the words), to his opponent's satisfaction.\n\nS.\n\nThe order must always be: first data (what, exactly, was actually said?), then interpretation of the data (what the speaker meant, not what the hear- er would have meant), then evaluation and argument (is he nght or wrong?). This might be called “constructionism,” for it is in explicit con- tradiction to the method that calls itself “Deconstructionism,” perhaps the most polar opposite to a Socratic debate in the entire history of philosophy.\n\n6.\n\nTwo formats are possible. (a) In the formal one, each debater is given a set amount of time to state, and his opponent to try to refute, his argument, tum by turn. E.g. A summanzes his argument in 5 minutes, then B has § minutes to respond, then A has 5 minutes to respond to B, then B gets another 5 minutes, etc., either for a pre-set amount of time or untl the moderator (or both debaters) decide they are “debated out.” (b) In the informal format, pre-set time structures are not imposed.\n\n7.\n\nWhether the time is explicitly counted and monitored or not, in both for- mats each debater must be given approximately equal time, or at least enough time to satisfy him and his need to explain himself.\n\n8.\n\nThe debate will usually work better with an impartial moderator, but it can sometimes work without one, if both parties adhere conscientiously to all the rules. Any two people can start a debate club, or a “Saint Socrates Socicty.” Try it! You might begin a Quiet Revolution C. Some rules about arguing logically:",
