          "role": "prose",
          "en": "Howto use Socratic method on difficult people But a “difficult person” (let us call him DP)is difficult precisely because he or she refuses this attitude of “let us try to find the truth together.” Either DP docs not believe in objective truth at all, and is a pnncipled subjectivist; or else he cannot or will not believe that he lacks this truth and his opponent has it (i.e.\n\nhe is an cgotist — which is usually a cover-up for a very fragile and threatened cgo, as the bully is the cover-up for the coward). The charm of the Socratic method is that it seems tailor-made for such people. For (1) it allows the truth to secp in under their defenses like subterranean water as they are busy erecting walls against the army of opposing ideas on the surface; and (2) it allows DP to be his own corrector, thus saving his ego. When you play Socrates you do not define the truth; you only ask questions and let DP define the truth with Ais answers, which then become your teacher which you explore and examine humbly, on the assumption that it is true, not false. (In the course of your exam- ination, of course, you discover that this assumption is questionable, and let us hopc that DP discovers this 100.) The art of Socratic dialogue resists being put into a universal and unchange- able formula, since you as Socrates must be flexible enough to follow DP wher- ever he takes you. And this is one of the keys to its usefulness with difficult peo- ple: it is in thus serving DP that you become the master, or rather allow truth to become the master.\n\nThus the Socratic method, used with a “difficult person.” is an illustration of the irony of Hegel's famous “master-slave dialectic,” in which the master is really the slave, enslaved to the slave he needs, while the slave is free and thus is really the master, for he does not need the master while the master needs him.",
