      "panels": [
        {
          "id": "p368-1",
          "speaker": "Page 368: XVI. Some Philosophical",
          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 368፦ XVI. Some Philosophical",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "XVI. Some Philosophical Applications of Logic Logic is not philosophy, but it is an excellent preparation for it. Logic is to phi- losophy whata telescope is to astronomyor a cookbook to a meal. It is an instru- ment. It is no substitute for the real thing, but it makes “the real thing” work much better.\n\nWhat follows is the briefest sample of the interface between logic and cach of the basic systematic divisions of philosophy:\n\nphilosophical theology, meta- physics.\n\ncosmology, philosophical anthropology.\n\nepistemology.",
          "am": "ይህ ክፍል በገጽ 368 ላይ የቀረበውን የሶክራታዊ ሎጂክ ዋና ሐሳብ ያካትታል። ሙሉው የእንግሊዝኛ ይዘት በትይዩ ተቀምጧል።",
          "notes": "Peter Kreeft, Socratic Logic (Ed. 3.1), Page 368."
        },
        {
          "id": "p368-2",
          "speaker": "Page 368 (Continued)",
          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 368 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "and ethics.\n\nThese divisions correspond to basic questions:\n\nabout God. or the Ultimate Reality; about reality as such, universally; about the visible universe; about human nature; about how we know: and about good and cvil, the good life and the good society. The following is only a sketchy sample of howlogic can make a difference to cach of these kinds of questions.\n\nSection 1. Logic and theology (P) In principle, what could possibly be a more important application of logic than its application to the ultimate question, the qucstion about ultimate reality?\n\nWhether there is an ultimate being, an absolute reality, and whatit is, must make a difference to cverything, for it is the ultimate standard or reference point for cverything, and also the ultimate end, “meaning of life,” summum bonum, or greatest good. Even if this is only pleasure or survival or money or sex or power - well, then, that is God and that is the ultimate and cverything else is relative to it. Even if it is nothingness, or meaninglessness — well, then, everything else is relative to that.\n\nIs it logically provable that there is a God, i.c. a being with at least some of the attributes ascribed to God by the world’s religions? An Infinitely Perfect Being? An Uncauscd Cause? A Creator? Can the use of the principles of formal logic that must be admitted by everyone plus sensory data available to everyone together supply premises from which the existence of God can be validly deduced in a way that should convince everyone? The most famous arguments",
          "am": "የገጽ 368 ሁለተኛ ክፍል ውይይት እና ማብራሪያ።",
          "notes": "Peter Kreeft, Socratic Logic (Ed. 3.1), Page 368 (Part 2)."
        }
      ]
