          "speakerAm": "ገጽ 375 (የቀጠለ)",
          "role": "prose",
          "en": "An even simpler truth-functional logical connective is negation. Letting *~' represent the word ‘not’, we get the following truth table for negation.\n\np “P casc | T F case 2 F T Here the atomic statement is p and the compound composed out of *~' and ‘p’ 1s “~p”. Now if we let ‘p’ be any meaningful assertion, then p must be either true or false. Whicheverit is, we can immediately figure out the value of “~p™. It is always the opposite of “p™. So negation is also truth functional.\n\nIt would be natural at this point to wonder whether all logic is truth-func- tional. A consideration in favor of an affirmative answer is the fact that other logical expressions can be built out of the previous two (in fact, in most mathe- matical logic textbooks all arc). Consider the logical connective ‘or’. Taking ‘or’ in the inclusive sense, that is, as a “weak disjunction” (see page 301), the state- ment “p or q” is true if cither *p’ or ‘q\" is, that is if either disjunct is. The only case in which an inclusive disjunction is false is that in which bothits disjuncts arc. Now we might represent that with the table following on page 366.\n\nBut there is no need to take ‘or’ as atomic. It can be built out of *and’ and ‘not’. To say that at least onc of ‘p’ or *q’ is true is to say that it’s not the casc",
