          "en": "First, whether or not modal logic is burdened by similar paradoxes,it is clearly a depar- ture from the attempt to have a purely truth functional logic. You cannot neces- sarily infer the truth value of “'p is necessary” from knowing whether p is true or false. For example. “You arc now reading a logic book,” is true, but it didn’t have to be. You could have gone on a walk instead (and perhaps should have).’ Secondly, modal logic turns out to support the basis of traditional logic.\n\nModal logician Alvin Plantinga, considering W.V.O. Quine’s accusation that modal logic implics “Aristotelian essentialism,” the view that things actually have essences, says that “Quine seems to be right ... [it] clearly does imply the truth ofthat ancient doctrine.”® The second way of responding to the paradoxes involves weakening the standard logic. It removes certain principles from basic modern logic, insisting that there must be some relevant connection between the antecedent and the con- sequent. This family of logics arc thus called relevance logics. Like modal log- ics, relevance logics are not purely truth functional.\n\nThere are many other kinds of systems of intensional logic: temporal logic, epistemic logic, deontic logic, intuitionist logic ¢f al. It is important to point out that whenever philosophers use advanced logical techniques to attempt to solve complex philosophical problems they always use one or more of these inten- sional systems. For every proposed solution on the basis of a system of inten- That's the C.1. Lewis with whom C.S. Lewis was once confused (sce page 12).\n\nThere is also inchminable intenstonality in the semantics of modal logic.\n\nAlvin Plantinga, The Nature of Necessity (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 1979), last page.",
