          "en": "Invalid. four terms as it stands.\n\nHowever, it 1s an cnthymeme with the implied premise “Whatever has three objects, is of three kinds,” and when this is added, we have two valid syllogisms.\n\nAll [existence] is [a perfection) No [perfections) arc [twi lacking in God] Therefore no [existence] is [twi lacking in God] : valid OR:\n\nAll [God] 1s [tw lacks no perfection) All [tw lacks no perfection] is [tw does not lack existence] Therefore all [God] is [tw docs not lack existence] : valid However, the argument merely proves that if the concept, or nature, or defini.\n\ntion, of God is to lack no perfection, and therefore not to lack existence cither, then we can conclude only that the concept of God cannot lack existence; we cannot conclude that God in fact exists. If we begin only with a concept (‘God’), we end only with a concept (‘God’); if we want to end with more than a concept (God without the quotation marks), we must begin with more than a concept (God without the quotation marks), but that would be begging the question in assuming a real God. You cannot prove God from ‘God® without committing the fallacy of four terms. You need other facts to prove the fact of God. A second problem with the argument is the confusion of existence with essence: perfec- tions are qualities of an essence. Existence is not a perfection but the precondi- tion for or actualization of all pcrfections.\n\nSome [twa south] 1s [twi west] And some [twi west] is [twi north] Therefore some [twi south] is [twi north] : invalid, Undistributed Middle and two particular premises. (“Can be’ means “Some 15.™) Page 271 2: All [tw makes the world go round) is [tw does not make it go flat) All {love] is [tw makes the world go round] Therefore all [love] is [tw does not make it go flat] : valid",
